# AGENTS.md — {{name}}

This is an **operonx** project (made with operonx {{version}}). The logic is
Python functions (ops), wired into graphs, which an application runs as
jobs{{#http}} and services{{/http}}.

## Before you write code

1. Read `.operonx/guide/README.md`. It indexes the guide of every installed
   operonx package (core, and operonx-agents or operonx-kb when added), and
   every example in them is tested against the installed version.
2. Before you use an operonx API, re-read the guide page that covers it.
   Do not write an operonx API from memory.
3. Read `app/main.py`. It is the map of the product.

## The ladder

- **op**: `@op` in `ops.py`. One step of logic; it returns a dict literal.
- **graph**: `@graph` in `graph.py`. Wires ops with `>>`. Its parameters
  are what it is given (a job item{{#http}}, a request body{{/http}}); the outputs of the
  ops wired to `END` are the answer.
- **Job**: in `jobs.py`. Runs a graph once per item of its `items` (a list,
  a function that yields them, or a `.jsonl` file) and keeps every result
  in its record. `reduce=` runs one graph over all the results; `steps=[...]`
  runs jobs in order as one command{{#http}}; `schedule=schedule(at="07:00")`
  also runs it on a clock inside `operonx serve`{{/http}}.
{{#http}}
- **Service**: in `routes.py`. A graph behind `http(...)`, `webhook(...)`,
  `schedule(...)` or `websocket(...)`.
{{/http}}
- **Application**: `APP` in `app/main.py`. Every job{{#http}} and service{{/http}} is
  declared there.
- **operonx.toml**: only points the CLIs at `APP`, plus `[tracing]`,
  `[retry]` and `[durable]`.

## Two rules the guide explains

- Every `@graph` is defined at module level in `graph.py`, never inside a
  function; a setting is a graph input, a different shape another graph.
{{#http}}
- A served graph's signature is the request: the body fills its parameters
  by name and the outputs wired to `END` are the reply. Never unpack a
  request dict in an op. Doors (`ingress()`) only for streams.
{{/http}}
{{#web}}
- The pages are `web/`, served at `/`; they call the server only through
  `web/lib/api.js`.
{{/web}}

## Layout

- One feature is one folder, `src/<feature>/`, with fixed file names:
  `graph.py` (wiring only: no logic, no I/O, no Python `if`), `ops.py` (the
  logic), `jobs.py`{{#http}}, `routes.py`{{/http}}{{#chat}}, `prompts.py`{{/chat}}{{#agent}}, `agent.py`{{/agent}}. Anything else is
  `_<name>.py`, private to the feature.
- What every feature needs goes in `src/_shared/`, which imports no
  feature. `src/_shared/config.py` is the only file that reads the
  environment.
- Imports go one way: `app/` → `graph.py` → `ops.py` → helpers. Nothing in
  `src/` imports `app/`. Another feature only through its `__init__` names
  or its graph.
- Runs read and keep files in `data/`, and write throwaway output to `out/`.
- Reach models and stores by their key in `resources.yaml`
  (`LLMOp.of(resource="assistant")`). Never build a client in code.
- Secrets live only in `.env`, as `${VAR}` in `resources.yaml`. Add every
  new name to `.env.example`. Never commit `.env`.
- Tests go in `tests/<feature>/`. Call an op directly (`my_op(x=1)()`), run
  a graph with a `Job`, and never call a real model: use a fake one.
{{brd}}
